<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peter Parler</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1333–<a href="/years/1399">1399</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Peter Parler was a German architect and sculptor, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-parler">Heinrich Parler</a>, who became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Emperor Charles IV</a>'s master builder in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> from 1356. He continued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-vitus-cathedral">St. Vitus Cathedral</a> with innovative net vaulting, built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-bridge">Charles Bridge</a> and its Old Town tower, and carved portrait busts in the cathedral's triforium. His workshop influenced late Gothic architecture across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peter-parler.jpg" alt="Peter Parler" width="320" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Packare · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Busta_Petr_Parl%C3%A9%C5%99.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Peter was born around 1333 in Schwäbisch Gmünd, where his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-parler">Heinrich Parler the Elder</a>, a stonemason from Cologne Cathedral, had been invited to take over the building of the parish church of the Holy Cross. Peter worked alongside his father when the cornerstone of the choir was laid in 1351. He then travelled as a journeyman to Cologne, perhaps to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and certainly to Strasbourg, whose cathedral he knew precisely. In Cologne he met Gertrud, daughter of the architect Bartholomäus von <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamm">Hamm</a>, and they married. Sometime after 1352 he joined the building of the Frauenkirche in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>, where the first sculptures can be directly ascribed to him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>By 1355 his style had come to the attention of Charles IV, who meant to make <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> the showcase capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>. Research usually places Peter's appointment in 1356. He took over the building of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-vitus-cathedral">St. Vitus Cathedral</a>, which had languished since the death of Matthias of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras">Arras</a> in 1352, and gradually changed the earlier plan. In 1357 Charles commissioned him to build a new bridge over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vltava">Vltava</a>. The first foundation stone was laid on 9 July 1357 at 5:31 am, a time chosen by the royal astrologers and fixed by the palindromic number 135797531 carved into the tower, and construction lasted until 1402.</p><p>He also built the Old Town Bridge Tower, whose arch holds the first net vault in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, and was the main designer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>'s New Town. His other works include the All Saints' Chapel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague-castle">Prague Castle</a> and the chancel of St. Bartholomew's church in Kolín. He was granted citizenship in Prague in 1379 and was still named as cathedral master on a panel at St. Vitus in 1396.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Peter's first wife Gertrude died in the late 1360s, and in 1380 he married Elisabeth Agnes von Bur. His son Johann Parler the Younger took over as master builder of St. Vitus in 1398, and Wenzel became master stonemason at St. Stephen's Cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. Peter died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> on 13 July 1399 and was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-vitus-cathedral">St. Vitus Cathedral</a>. The designs of his father and himself became known as the Parler style and spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>, with examples at St. Martin's Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landshut">Landshut</a>. In the Hussite wars of 1419–1434 much of his sculpture was destroyed, though his buildings stand.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Peter Parler” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The university he founded in 1348, today Charles University, was the oldest in Central Europe, and the Gothic architecture of <b>Peter Parler</b> at St Vitus Cathedral and on the Charles Bridge, together with the castle of Karlštejn, shaped the art of the whole region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Heinrich Parler began the Holy Cross Minster there in the 14th century, one of the first German hall churches in the late Gothic style, and his son <b>Peter Parler</b> went on to build Prague Cathedral.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-schwabisch-gmund">Free Imperial City of Schwäbisch Gmünd</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Schwäbisch Gmünd, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.8,9.8&amp;year=1375" aria-label="Schwäbisch Gmünd on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4777 460 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Peter Parler"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5227 730h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5227 730h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.8,9.8&amp;year=1375">What was here in 1375? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
